NAME
    step - single-step instructions, step over a call, or step out

SYNOPSIS
    step [<count>]
    s [<count>]
    step over
    step out
    step <cpu> [<count>|over|out]

DESCRIPTION
    Advances execution from a halt and halts again once the requested step
    completes. 's' is registered as a direct alias for 'step'. Any active
    breakpoint reached before the step count finishes still halts early.

    Prefixing a processor name from cpus(1) steps that processor while
    other processors continue running on the shared timebase. 'over' and
    'out' require a processor that advertises a call stack ('bt' in
    cpus(1), such as main CPU and SA-1 on SNES).

OPTIONS
    <count>
        Execute <count> instructions (parsed as hexadecimal; default 1).

    over
        Run a call at the current instruction to completion and halt on
        the next instruction at the original call-stack depth (safe across
        recursion; acts like a 1-instruction step on non-call opcodes).

    out
        Run until the current routine returns to its caller (call-stack
        depth - 1). Fails if the tracked call stack is empty.

EXAMPLES
    step
    s 20
    step over
    step out
    step sa1 out

EXIT STATUS
    0   Step armed and resume requested.
    1   No target attached, empty call stack on 'out', unsupported
        processor, invalid hex count, or session service fault.

SEE ALSO
    cpus(1), bt(1), runto(1), bp(1), resume(1)
